---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'three', label: 'The three targets' },
   { id: 'default', label: 'Default selection' },
   { id: 'virtual', label: 'Requesting virtual' },
   { id: 'real', label: 'Running a real target' },
   { id: 'choose', label: 'Which to use' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Targets"
   summary="A target is the screen reader a driver session controls. VoiceOver and NVDA drive the real software and return its speech. The virtual target simulates a reader in memory. sr start takes --sr to pick one, and the result says which kind you got."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="three">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Targets</p>
         <h2>The three targets</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>voiceover</code>
            <span>
               macOS. Drives VoiceOver through Guidepup and returns its speech. Supports screen
               recording to a <code>.mov</code> file. <code>targetType</code> is <code>real</code>.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>nvda</code>
            <span>
               Windows. Drives NVDA through Guidepup and returns its speech. Supports screen
               recording to an <code>.mp4</code> file. <code>targetType</code> is <code>real</code>.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>virtual</code>
            <span>
               Any platform. Runs Guidepup's virtual screen reader inside a headless Chromium page
               when the session opens a URL and a Chromium-family browser is installed, and inside a
               jsdom document otherwise. No window on screen, no recording. <code>targetType</code>
               is <code>simulated</code>, and <code>engine</code> is <code>browser</code> or
               <code>jsdom</code>.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Every session response carries <code>target</code> and <code>targetType</code>. Read
         <code>targetType</code> before you use the output as evidence.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="default">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Defaults</p>
            <h2>What you get without --sr</h2>
         </div>
         <p>a11ied picks a target from the operating system, then checks that it is ready:</p>
         <ol>
            <li>macOS picks <code>voiceover</code>. Windows picks <code>nvda</code>.</li>
            <li>Linux and every other platform fall back to <code>virtual</code>.</li>
            <li>
               If the platform reader is present but not ready for automation, the session falls back
               to <code>virtual</code> and the response explains why.
            </li>
         </ol>
         <p>
            Every fallback to <code>virtual</code> attaches a warning to the response. The warning
            says the reader is simulated and the output does not reflect real assistive technology.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Check readiness</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>a1 doctor</code> reports each target as <code>ready</code> or
            <code>requires-setup</code>, with the command to run.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="virtual">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Virtual</p>
         <h2>Ask for the virtual target on purpose</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Naming <code>--sr virtual</code> is not enough when a real reader is available. Add
         <code>--allow-virtual</code> to confirm you want the simulation.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Pass the URL so the reader has a page to read. Nothing opens on screen: the session loads
         the page in a headless Chromium page and injects the virtual reader into it, so the page's
         own scripts run and a live region a script fills or a dialog a click opens is announced.
         <code>a1 sr status</code> reports the engine, and so does <code>session.engine</code> in
         every JSON result. Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium on the machine is used first, then
         Playwright's Chromium from <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Two cases use jsdom instead, an in-memory document where page scripts do not run: a
         session started without a URL, which reads a placeholder document until
         <code>sr open</code>, and a machine with no Chromium-family browser, where the session
         falls back and reports <code>engine: "jsdom"</code>. Inline HTML passed to the test API
         renders in jsdom too. Needing no window is what makes this target usable in CI and in
         fast local loops.
      </p>
      <p>
         MCP clients pass the same two fields: <code>target: "virtual"</code> and
         <code>allowVirtual: true</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="real">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Real targets</p>
            <h2>Open the page before you start</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            VoiceOver and NVDA read whatever is already on screen. Give <code>sr start</code> the
            URL and it opens the page in the system browser, waits, and focuses that window before
            the session begins.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr next --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>sr start</code> opens the URL in the system browser and focuses that window, and
            records which app it opened. A bare <code>a1 sr focus</code> brings that app back to
            the front later. <code>sr focus</code> also accepts <code>--app</code> or
            <code>--bundle-id</code> on macOS, <code>--process</code> or <code>--pid</code> on
            Windows, and <code>--window-title</code> on either.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">One time setup</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Run <code>a1 setup</code> before the first real session on a machine. It runs
            Guidepup's <code>setup</code> and <code>install</code> commands and re-checks with
            <code>doctor</code>. See <a href="/install#screen-readers">install</a>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="choose">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Choosing</p>
         <h2>Which target answers the question</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Use <code>virtual</code> to check announcement order, names, roles, and states from the
         accessibility tree. It is deterministic and needs no window, so it belongs in the loop you
         run on every change.
      </p>
      <p>
         Use <code>voiceover</code> or <code>nvda</code> when the answer depends on the real
         software: rotor behavior, interaction mode, focus handling in dialogs, and anything you plan
         to record or quote in a bug report.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
